      "text": "### Author Background and Publication Context\n\n**Ama Owusu** is a debut author born in Kumasi, Ghana, in 1990. She holds a BA in English and Linguistics from the University of Ghana (2012) and completed an MFA in Creative Writing at the University of East Anglia (2015). Her short fiction has been published in notable outlets, including Granta and Transition Magazine. **Glassy-eyed**, released by Cassava Press in January 2023, is her debut novel and was longlisted for the Etisalat Prize for Literature. The book focuses on three generations of the Boateng-Asante family in contemporary Accra, centered around a glass-blower's workshop, which serves as a metaphor for inheritance and resilience amidst changing times.\n\nFor more details on Owusu's background, you can read her author profile [here](https://openbooks.net/authors/ama-owusu) and an interview discussing her writing process [here](https://openbooks.net/interviews/ama-owusu).\n\n### Critics and Early Reviewer Reactions\n\nEarly reviews of **Glassy-eyed** have been largely positive. The **Africa Report** describes it as “an assured, quietly devastating portrait of an Accra family across three generations,” highlighting the glass-blower's workshop as both a literal and metaphorical centerpiece of the story. The review commends Owusu's precise and unhurried prose, noting her attention to the weight of objects and the silences that come with inheritance. This debut is recognized as one of the most impressive from West Africa in years.\n\nAdditional insights on its publication can be found [here](https://openbooks.net/books/glassy-eyed).\n\n### Context in Contemporary Ghanaian/West African Fiction\n\n**Glassy-eyed** fits into the broader landscape of contemporary Ghanaian and West African fiction by addressing themes of family, tradition, and craftsmanship. This novel stands out for its focus on the intersection of personal and cultural history, as seen in the multi-generational structure that reflects the lives and legacies within the Boateng-Asante family. Owusu's work adds to the conversation of how modern African narratives can explore deeply rooted cultural practices and the significance of inheritance in a rapidly changing world. The book's success and recognition, including its longlisting for the Etisalat Prize and shortlisting for the Commonwealth Writers Prize (Africa region) in 2024, further mark its importance in the literary canon.\n\nYou can explore more about the thematic emphases of the novel on the publisher's page [here](https://openbooks.net/books/glassy-eyed).",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
  ],
